1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,887
Welcome to episode seven.

2
00:00:01,927 --> 00:00:07,204
Real reactions rarely get reactants
in exactly the right proportions.

3
00:00:07,244 --> 00:00:12,137
One reactant runs out first, and
some of the other is left over.

4
00:00:12,177 --> 00:00:16,030
Today we'll learn to work
out which, and how much.

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,554
Two quick checks.

6
00:00:17,594 --> 00:00:20,574
Balance the complete
combustion of methane.

7
00:00:20,614 --> 00:00:26,123
And what volume does zero point one zero
zero moles of carbon dioxide occupy at

8
00:00:26,163 --> 00:00:28,300
standard laboratory conditions?

9
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:29,534
Pause and answer.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,442
Methane plus two oxygen forms
carbon dioxide plus two water.

11
00:00:42,482 --> 00:00:48,642
And zero point one zero zero moles times
twenty-four point eight litres per mole is

12
00:00:48,682 --> 00:00:50,774
two point four eight litres.

13
00:00:51,733 --> 00:00:56,048
Think of the balanced equation
as a recipe for one batch:

14
00:00:56,088 --> 00:00:58,875
one methane and two oxygen molecules.

15
00:00:58,915 --> 00:01:03,918
Suppose we have three methane
molecules and four oxygen molecules.

16
00:01:03,958 --> 00:01:06,210
Methane is the smaller number.

17
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:08,771
Is methane the one that runs out?

18
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:10,715
Let's build batches.

19
00:01:10,755 --> 00:01:14,363
Each batch takes one
methane and two oxygens.

20
00:01:14,403 --> 00:01:16,147
The first batch: fine.

21
00:01:16,187 --> 00:01:18,012
The second batch: fine.

22
00:01:18,052 --> 00:01:22,228
Now we're out of oxygen,
with one methane left over.

23
00:01:22,268 --> 00:01:27,539
So oxygen limited the reaction,
even though there was more of it.

24
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:29,887
The reason is the recipe.

25
00:01:29,927 --> 00:01:33,795
Each batch needs twice as
much oxygen as methane.

26
00:01:33,835 --> 00:01:38,102
So what matters isn't the
raw amount of each reactant.

27
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:42,728
It's how many batches each
reactant could make on its own:

28
00:01:42,768 --> 00:01:45,799
its amount divided by its coefficient.

29
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:47,824
Scale this up to moles.

30
00:01:47,864 --> 00:01:53,995
With one point zero zero moles of methane
and one point five zero moles of oxygen,

31
00:01:54,035 --> 00:01:58,209
methane could make one point
zero zero moles of batches,

32
00:01:58,249 --> 00:02:01,068
but oxygen only zero point seven five.

33
00:02:01,108 --> 00:02:06,637
The smaller value of amount divided by
coefficient identifies the limiting

34
00:02:06,677 --> 00:02:07,956
reactant: oxygen.

35
00:02:08,933 --> 00:02:14,625
The smaller value, zero point seven five
moles, is called the reaction extent:

36
00:02:14,665 --> 00:02:18,667
the number of moles of the
recipe that actually happen.

37
00:02:18,707 --> 00:02:21,059
Everything else follows from it.

38
00:02:21,399 --> 00:02:24,816
Amount used equals
coefficient times extent.

39
00:02:24,856 --> 00:02:30,944
Methane: one times zero point seven five,
so zero point seven five moles used.

40
00:02:30,984 --> 00:02:36,837
Oxygen: two times zero point seven
five, so one point five zero moles used,

41
00:02:36,877 --> 00:02:41,748
which is all of it, as it should
be for the limiting reactant.

42
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:44,262
Products form the same way.

43
00:02:44,302 --> 00:02:50,690
Carbon dioxide: one times zero point seven
five, so zero point seven five moles.

44
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:56,274
Water: two times zero point seven
five, so one point five zero moles.

45
00:02:56,666 --> 00:02:59,913
Finally, remaining equals
initial minus used.

46
00:02:59,953 --> 00:03:05,974
Methane: one point zero zero minus zero
point seven five leaves zero point two five

47
00:03:06,014 --> 00:03:07,143
moles in excess.

48
00:03:07,183 --> 00:03:11,963
Oxygen: one point five zero minus
one point five zero leaves zero.

49
00:03:12,003 --> 00:03:13,935
Check: nothing is negative.

50
00:03:13,975 --> 00:03:20,037
A negative remaining amount always means
the wrong reactant was chosen as limiting.

51
00:03:20,399 --> 00:03:21,690
Quick prediction.

52
00:03:21,730 --> 00:03:28,185
This time, zero point two zero moles of
methane meets zero point five zero moles of

53
00:03:28,225 --> 00:03:28,733
oxygen.

54
00:03:28,773 --> 00:03:33,584
Which reactant is limiting, and
how much of the other is left?

55
00:03:33,624 --> 00:03:38,085
Pause and work it out with
amount divided by coefficient.

56
00:03:48,533 --> 00:03:53,457
Methane: zero point two zero
over one is zero point two zero.

57
00:03:53,497 --> 00:03:58,421
Oxygen: zero point five zero
over two is zero point two five.

58
00:03:58,461 --> 00:04:02,734
Methane is smaller, so this
time methane is limiting,

59
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:06,315
with an extent of zero
point two zero moles.

60
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:12,988
Oxygen used is two times zero point two
zero, which is zero point four zero moles,

61
00:04:13,028 --> 00:04:16,569
leaving zero point one
zero moles of oxygen.

62
00:04:16,609 --> 00:04:21,655
The same two reactants can swap
roles when the amounts change.

63
00:04:22,598 --> 00:04:26,908
Practice question Q zero
thirteen is worth six marks.

64
00:04:26,948 --> 00:04:29,165
Pause the video and try it.

65
00:04:44,598 --> 00:04:49,968
This is an idealised calculation: the
ethanol that reacts burns completely,

66
00:04:50,008 --> 00:04:53,430
and any excess ethanol is
simply left unreacted.

67
00:04:53,470 --> 00:04:55,162
First, amounts in moles.

68
00:04:55,202 --> 00:04:59,490
Ethanol is already given as zero
point four zero zero moles.

69
00:04:59,530 --> 00:05:05,477
Oxygen: thirty-two point zero grams divided
by thirty-two point zero grams per mole

70
00:05:05,517 --> 00:05:07,609
is one point zero zero moles.

71
00:05:07,998 --> 00:05:13,775
The balanced equation: ethanol plus three
oxygen forms two carbon dioxide plus

72
00:05:13,815 --> 00:05:14,670
three water.

73
00:05:14,710 --> 00:05:17,803
Now compare amount divided by coefficient.

74
00:05:17,843 --> 00:05:23,247
Ethanol: zero point four zero zero
over one is zero point four zero zero.

75
00:05:23,287 --> 00:05:28,543
Oxygen: one point zero zero over
three is zero point three three three.

76
00:05:28,583 --> 00:05:32,610
Oxygen gives the smaller
value, so oxygen is limiting.

77
00:05:32,998 --> 00:05:39,844
Another way to say it: zero point four zero
zero moles of ethanol would need three

78
00:05:39,884 --> 00:05:43,792
times as much oxygen,
one point two zero moles,

79
00:05:43,832 --> 00:05:46,899
and we only have one point zero zero.

80
00:05:46,939 --> 00:05:52,986
That sentence is a good way to justify
the limiting reactant in an exam.

81
00:05:53,331 --> 00:05:56,604
The reaction extent is
one third of a mole.

82
00:05:56,644 --> 00:06:01,842
Ethanol used is one third, about
zero point three three three moles.

83
00:06:01,882 --> 00:06:08,159
Ethanol remaining is zero point four zero
zero minus zero point three three three,

84
00:06:08,199 --> 00:06:11,626
which is zero point zero
six six seven moles.

85
00:06:11,666 --> 00:06:14,861
Times forty-six point zero grams per mole,

86
00:06:14,901 --> 00:06:19,292
that's three point zero seven
grams of ethanol left over.

87
00:06:19,664 --> 00:06:24,910
Carbon dioxide formed is two times the
extent: zero point six six seven moles.

88
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:29,383
Times forty-four point zero, that's
twenty-nine point three grams.

89
00:06:29,423 --> 00:06:34,940
And the energy released is the extent times
one thousand three hundred and seventy

90
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:39,752
kilojoules per mole of ethanol: four
hundred and fifty-six point seven,

91
00:06:39,792 --> 00:06:42,909
about four hundred and
fifty-seven kilojoules.

92
00:06:43,264 --> 00:06:47,398
Marks: two for the amounts and
the limiting justification,

93
00:06:47,438 --> 00:06:51,932
two for ethanol used and remaining,
one for the carbon dioxide,

94
00:06:51,972 --> 00:06:53,587
and one for the energy.

95
00:06:53,627 --> 00:06:54,307
Two traps.

96
00:06:54,347 --> 00:07:00,208
Choosing ethanol as limiting because zero
point four zero zero is smaller than one

97
00:07:00,248 --> 00:07:03,159
point zero zero ignores the coefficients.

98
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:09,060
And reporting zero point four zero zero
moles as the amount remaining confuses the

99
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,475
starting amount with what's left.

100
00:07:12,464 --> 00:07:14,594
Gases often arrive as mixtures.

101
00:07:14,634 --> 00:07:19,914
Air is about twenty-one percent oxygen
by volume; the rest, mostly nitrogen,

102
00:07:19,954 --> 00:07:22,434
doesn't react in these calculations.

103
00:07:22,474 --> 00:07:26,424
A fuel stream might be methane
mixed with carbon dioxide,

104
00:07:26,464 --> 00:07:28,494
which can't burn any further.

105
00:07:28,864 --> 00:07:32,677
For ideal gases at the same
temperature and pressure,

106
00:07:32,717 --> 00:07:35,295
equal volumes contain equal amounts.

107
00:07:35,335 --> 00:07:38,567
So a volume fraction is
also a mole fraction.

108
00:07:38,607 --> 00:07:42,420
That's an assumption, and
questions usually state it.

109
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:48,204
If the gases are at different conditions,
you can't use the fractions this way.

110
00:07:48,597 --> 00:07:52,461
So split each stream before
you do any stoichiometry.

111
00:07:52,501 --> 00:07:57,028
From the air, only the oxygen
fraction goes into the reaction.

112
00:07:57,068 --> 00:08:00,564
From the fuel, only the
methane fraction reacts.

113
00:08:00,604 --> 00:08:04,214
The nitrogen and the carbon
dioxide pass through.

114
00:08:05,197 --> 00:08:08,888
If the fuel stream already
contains carbon dioxide,

115
00:08:08,928 --> 00:08:11,375
the exhaust has two sources of it.

116
00:08:11,415 --> 00:08:15,692
Some carbon dioxide is newly
formed by burning the methane.

117
00:08:15,732 --> 00:08:20,633
The rest simply entered with the
fuel and passed through unchanged.

118
00:08:20,997 --> 00:08:23,632
A question might ask for either one.

119
00:08:23,672 --> 00:08:28,239
"Carbon dioxide formed" or
"produced" means only the new part,

120
00:08:28,279 --> 00:08:30,394
calculated from the reaction.

121
00:08:30,434 --> 00:08:34,778
"Total carbon dioxide in the
exhaust" means new plus inlet.

122
00:08:34,818 --> 00:08:38,682
Read which one is asked for
before you add anything.

123
00:08:39,662 --> 00:08:43,422
Practice question Q zero
fourteen is worth eight marks.

124
00:08:43,462 --> 00:08:48,298
Pause the video and work through the
whole question before continuing.

125
00:09:03,729 --> 00:09:05,701
Start with the fuel stream.

126
00:09:05,741 --> 00:09:11,809
Twelve point four litres at standard
laboratory conditions is zero point five zero

127
00:09:11,849 --> 00:09:13,150
zero moles of gas.

128
00:09:13,190 --> 00:09:17,546
Eighty percent is methane: zero
point four zero zero moles.

129
00:09:17,586 --> 00:09:23,844
Twenty percent is carbon dioxide: zero point
one zero zero moles, which won't react.

130
00:09:24,196 --> 00:09:25,050
Now the air.

131
00:09:25,090 --> 00:09:31,013
Twenty-one point zero percent of ninety
point zero litres is eighteen point nine

132
00:09:31,053 --> 00:09:32,280
litres of oxygen.

133
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:34,889
Divided by twenty-four point eight,

134
00:09:34,929 --> 00:09:38,805
that's zero point seven six
two one moles of oxygen.

135
00:09:39,196 --> 00:09:42,293
The equation is methane plus two oxygen.

136
00:09:42,333 --> 00:09:48,647
Amount divided by coefficient: methane
zero point four zero zero over one is zero

137
00:09:48,687 --> 00:09:50,294
point four zero zero.

138
00:09:50,334 --> 00:09:56,569
Oxygen zero point seven six two one over
two is zero point three eight one zero.

139
00:09:56,609 --> 00:09:59,785
Oxygen is smaller, so oxygen is limiting,

140
00:09:59,825 --> 00:10:04,923
and the reaction extent is zero
point three eight one zero moles.

141
00:10:05,262 --> 00:10:07,827
Keep the two budgets side by side.

142
00:10:07,867 --> 00:10:13,726
Oxygen: initial zero point seven six
two one, used all of it, remaining zero.

143
00:10:13,766 --> 00:10:19,931
Methane: initial zero point four zero
zero, used zero point three eight one zero,

144
00:10:19,971 --> 00:10:23,914
remaining zero point zero
one eight nine five moles.

145
00:10:23,954 --> 00:10:26,978
Times sixteen point zero grams per mole,

146
00:10:27,018 --> 00:10:31,998
that's zero point three zero three
grams of methane left unburnt.

147
00:10:32,995 --> 00:10:36,990
Newly formed carbon dioxide
equals the methane that reacted:

148
00:10:37,030 --> 00:10:39,546
zero point three eight one zero moles.

149
00:10:39,586 --> 00:10:44,293
At standard laboratory conditions,
that's nine point four five litres.

150
00:10:44,661 --> 00:10:48,998
The inlet carbon dioxide was
zero point one zero zero moles,

151
00:10:49,038 --> 00:10:51,260
or two point four eight litres.

152
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:56,877
So the total carbon dioxide, once isolated
at standard laboratory conditions,

153
00:10:56,917 --> 00:11:02,348
is nine point four five plus two point
four eight: eleven point nine three,

154
00:11:02,388 --> 00:11:04,650
about eleven point nine litres.

155
00:11:04,995 --> 00:11:10,443
Energy released: zero point three eight
one zero moles of methane times eight

156
00:11:10,483 --> 00:11:13,222
hundred and ninety kilojoules per mole,

157
00:11:13,262 --> 00:11:16,786
which is three hundred and
thirty-nine kilojoules.

158
00:11:16,826 --> 00:11:21,673
That value is for water formed as
a liquid, matching the data given.

159
00:11:22,061 --> 00:11:26,507
Marks: two for the reactive fuel
amount and the oxygen amount,

160
00:11:26,547 --> 00:11:30,920
one for the limiting decision,
two for the remaining methane,

161
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:35,663
one each for new carbon dioxide,
total carbon dioxide and energy.

162
00:11:36,061 --> 00:11:37,643
Three traps to watch.

163
00:11:37,683 --> 00:11:43,977
Treating all twelve point four litres of
fuel gas as methane gives zero point five

164
00:11:44,017 --> 00:11:46,062
zero zero moles of methane.

165
00:11:46,102 --> 00:11:52,472
Treating all ninety litres of air as oxygen
gives far too much oxygen and the wrong

166
00:11:52,512 --> 00:11:53,863
limiting reactant.

167
00:11:53,903 --> 00:11:58,033
And calling the nine point
four five litres the total,

168
00:11:58,073 --> 00:12:02,358
or the eleven point nine three
litres the amount formed,

169
00:12:02,398 --> 00:12:05,333
mixes up new and inlet carbon dioxide.

170
00:12:06,328 --> 00:12:07,103
The routine.

171
00:12:07,143 --> 00:12:12,403
One: convert everything to moles, using
only the reactive part of any mixture.

172
00:12:12,443 --> 00:12:17,092
Two: divide each amount by its
coefficient; the smallest is limiting,

173
00:12:17,132 --> 00:12:19,130
and gives the reaction extent.

174
00:12:19,170 --> 00:12:22,120
Three: used equals
coefficient times extent.

175
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:26,061
Four: remaining equals initial
minus used, never negative.

176
00:12:26,101 --> 00:12:29,662
Five: products formed equal
coefficient times extent,

177
00:12:29,702 --> 00:12:32,488
plus anything that simply passes through.

178
00:12:32,861 --> 00:12:34,020
Closing recall.

179
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:40,096
Three point zero moles of hydrogen and
two point zero moles of oxygen react:

180
00:12:40,136 --> 00:12:43,373
two hydrogen plus oxygen forms two water.

181
00:12:43,413 --> 00:12:47,650
Which is limiting, and how
much of the other is left?

182
00:12:58,061 --> 00:13:02,348
Hydrogen: three point zero
over two is one point five.

183
00:13:02,388 --> 00:13:06,354
Oxygen: two point zero over
one is two point zero.

184
00:13:06,394 --> 00:13:11,242
Hydrogen is limiting, with an
extent of one point five moles.

185
00:13:11,282 --> 00:13:14,126
Oxygen used is one point five moles,

186
00:13:14,166 --> 00:13:17,972
so zero point five zero
moles of oxygen remains.

187
00:13:18,012 --> 00:13:24,743
Next episode: measuring the energy from a
fuel, and how efficient heating really is.
